We keep a small catalogue of products in a hash, keyed by SKU.

use strict;
use warnings;

Prices are held in whole cents, so here is a helper that turns a number of
cents into a dollar string.

sub price_str {
    my ($cents) = @_;
    return sprintf('$%d.%02d', $cents / 100, $cents % 100);
}

Each product has a name, a price and a list of tags.

my %catalogue = (
    "CF-100" => { name => "Coffee Beans", price => 1299, tags => [qw(pantry hot)] },
    "MG-200" => { name => "Ceramic Mug",  price => 850,  tags => [qw(kitchen)] },
);

Now we list everything, from the cheapest upward, and count the kitchen
things along the way.

my @skus = sort keys %catalogue;
my $kitchen = 0;

foreach my $sku (@skus) {
    my $product = $catalogue{$sku};
    $kitchen++ if grep { $_ eq "kitchen" } @{ $product->{tags} };
    printf "%-6s %-14s %8s\n", $sku, $product->{name}, price_str($product->{price});
}

Finally we report the count.

print "Kitchen items: $kitchen\n";
